Hitchhiking is legal in Ireland and no law forbids asking for a lift; motorways are the exception and the rule there is unusually blunt. [https://www.irishstatutebook.ie/eli/1993/act/14/section/43/enacted/en/html Section 43(4)(a) of the Roads Act 1993] states simply that "pedestrians and pedal cyclists shall not use a motorway", and section 43(5)(b) makes contravening it an offence — that covers the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads of every M-road. Motorway service areas are reached off the motorway rather than being part of it, so asking drivers there is lawful, though the operator can ask you to leave. National and regional roads — the N and R network, including the dual carriageways that are not motorways — carry no such prohibition, and that is where hitchhiking in Ireland is done. Stand off the carriageway, on the verge, where a driver can see you and pull in safely.
